arXiv:2506.23235v1 [cs.CL] 29 Jun 2025</div>
</div><div class="ltx_page_content">
<article class="ltx_document ltx_authors_1line">
<h1 class="ltx_title ltx_title_document">Generalist Reward Models: Found Inside Large Language Models
</h1>
<div class="ltx_authors">
<span class="ltx_creator ltx_role_author">
<span class="ltx_personname">Yi-Chen Li<sup id="id1" class="ltx_sup">*</sup>
</span></span>
<span class="ltx_author_before">  </span><span class="ltx_creator ltx_role_author">
<span class="ltx_personname">Tian Xu<sup id="id2" class="ltx_sup">*</sup>
</span></span>
<span class="ltx_author_before">  </span><span class="ltx_creator ltx_role_author">
<span class="ltx_personname">Yang Yu
</span><span class="ltx_author_notes"><span class="ltx_author_notes_content">
<span class="ltx_contact ltx_role_affiliation"><span class="ltx_contact_name">Affiliation: </span> Correspondence: <span id="id3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">yuy@nju.edu.cn</span>
</span></span></span></span>
<span class="ltx_author_before">  </span><span class="ltx_creator ltx_role_author">
<span class="ltx_personname">Xuqin Zhang
</span></span>
<span class="ltx_author_before">  </span><span class="ltx_creator ltx_role_author">
<span class="ltx_personname">Xiong-Hui Chen<span id="id4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Zhongxiang Ling, Ningjing Chao, Lei Yuan, Zhi-Hua Zhou</span>National Key Laboratory for Novel Software Technology, Nanjing University, ChinaSchool of Artificial Intelligence, Nanjing University, China<sup id="id5" class="ltx_sup">*</sup> Equal contribution
</span></span></div>

<div id="abstract1" class="ltx_abstract"><h6 class="ltx_title ltx_title_abstract">Abstract</h6>
    
<p id="abstract1.1" class="ltx_p">The alignment of Large Language Models (LLMs) is critically dependent on reward models trained on costly human preference data. While recent work explores bypassing this cost with AI feedback, these methods often lack a rigorous theoretical foundation. In this paper, we discover that a powerful generalist reward model is already latently present within any LLM trained via standard next-token prediction. We prove that this <em id="abstract1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">endogenous reward</em> is not a heuristic, but is theoretically equivalent to a reward function learned through offline inverse reinforcement learning. This connection allows us to directly elicit a high-quality reward signal from a base (pre-trained or supervised fine-tuned) model without any further training. Critically, we also prove that subsequent reinforcement learning using this endogenous reward leads to a policy with a provably superior error bound compared to the base model. To our best knowledge, this is the first theoretical proof of the effectiveness of reinforcement learning for LLMs. Our experiments validate this theory, demonstrating that our method not only outperforms existing LLM-as-a-judge approaches but can also surpass explicitly trained reward models.</p>
    
<p id="abstract1.2" class="ltx_p">These findings suggest that the reward modeling stage can be replaced by a principled method of eliciting the knowledge already captured during pre-training, heralding a more efficient, powerful, and scalable paradigm for LLMs alignment as well as multi-modal models.</p>
  
</div>
<div id="id6" class="ltx_logical-block ltx_align_bottom">
    <span class="ltx_rule" style="width:100%;height:2.0pt;--ltx-bg-color:black;display:inline-block;"> </span>
    <span class="ltx_rule" style="width:100%;height:2.0pt;--ltx-bg-color:black;display:inline-block;"> </span>
    
<div id="p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">A Preprint
<br class="ltx_break"></span></p>
<table id="p1.2" class="ltx_tabular ltx_align_top">
<tr id="p1.2.1" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="p1.2.1.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center"><span class="ltx_rule" style="width:0.0pt;height:24.0pt;--ltx-bg-color:black;display:inline-block;"></span></td></tr>
</table>
</div>
  
</div>
<section id="S1" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">1 </span>Introduction</h2>

<div id="S1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Aligning Large Language Models (LLMs) with complex human values, such as helpfulness and honesty, remains a central challenge in AI development. The predominant paradigm for this task is Reinforcement Learning from Human Feedback (RLHF) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib7" title="" class="ltx_ref">Christiano et al., 2017</a>; <a href="#bib.bib2" title="" class="ltx_ref">Bai et al., 2022a</a>; <a href="#bib.bib27" title="" class="ltx_ref">OpenAI, 2022</a>)</cite>. This pipeline critically relies on a reward model (RM) trained on human preferences to score model outputs, and the quality of the final, aligned LLM is fundamentally determined by the quality of this RM. Consequently, creating a state-of-the-art RM has previously required building massive, high-quality human preference datasets, which is notoriously slow, expensive, and difficult to scale.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">This dependency on human labeling has spurred research into alternative alignment methods. A prominent direction is Reinforcement Learning from AI Feedback (RLAIF) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib3" title="" class="ltx_ref">Bai et al., 2022b</a>; <a href="#bib.bib30" title="" class="ltx_ref">Pang et al., 2023</a>; <a href="#bib.bib17" title="" class="ltx_ref">Lee et al., 2024a</a>)</cite> or the “LLM-as-a-judge” framework <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib48" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zheng et al., 2023</a>; <a href="#bib.bib12" title="" class="ltx_ref">Gu et al., 2025</a>)</cite>, which uses a powerful proprietary LLM to generate reward signals or preference labels, thus bypassing the need for human annotators. While cost-effective, these methods are often <em id="S1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">heuristic</em>; they lack a rigorous theoretical foundation and can be susceptible to inheriting the stylistic quirks and biases of the judge model. This raises a critical question: must a high-quality reward signal be sourced externally at all?</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">This paper discover that a powerful generalist reward model is not something that needs to be built, but rather something that can be <em id="S1.p3.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">uncovered</em>, as it is already latently present within any language model trained via standard next-token prediction. We term this the <span id="S1.p3.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">endogenous reward</span>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.p4.1" class="ltx_p">Our core contribution is to provide a rigorous theoretical basis for this claim. We demonstrate that the reward function from a specific form of offline inverse reinforcement learning (IRL) can be recovered from the standard next-token prediction objective, used for both pre-training and supervised fine-tuning (SFT). This insight allows us to move beyond heuristics and establish a principled method for eliciting the reward function implicitly learned by the language model during its training. Specifically, we show that a language model’s logits can be directly interpreted as the soft Q-function, from which a reward function can be recovered via the inverse soft Bellman operator.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p5" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.p5.1" class="ltx_p">Crucially, this theoretical connection provides more than just a method for reward extraction. We prove that fine-tuning a model with its own endogenous reward leads to a policy with a provably superior error bound compared to the base model. The RL process effectively corrects for the <em id="S1.p5.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">compounding errors</em> that plague standard imitation learning (i.e., next-token prediction), reducing the performance gap from a quadratic dependence on the task horizon, <math id="S1.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{O}}(H^{2})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{O}}(H^{2})</annotation></semantics></math>, to a superior linear one, <math id="S1.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{O}}(H)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{O}}(H)</annotation></semantics></math>. To our best knowledge, this is the first theoretical proof of the effectiveness of reinforcement learning for LLMs.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p6" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.p6.1" class="ltx_p">Our extensive experiments validate this theory, showing that eliciting this endogenous reward is not only superior to existing LLM-as-a-judge methods but can also surpass the performance of reward models trained explicitly on expensive human-labeled data. These findings imply that the reward modeling stage can potentially be replaced by a principled process of eliciting the knowledge already captured by the base model, heralding a more efficient, powerful, and theoretically grounded paradigm for LLM alignment.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">2 </span>Preliminaries</h2>

<section id="S2.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">2.1 </span>LLM and its MDP Formulation</h3>

<section id="S2.SS1.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Basic Introduction on LLM.</h4>

<div id="S2.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">A Large Language Model (LLM) is a generative model that operates by predicting the next token in a sequence by probabilistic modeling. Formally, an LLM <math id="S2.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>π</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi</annotation></semantics></math> selects tokens from a finite vocabulary <math id="S2.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{V}}=\{1,2,\ldots,|{\mathcal{V}}|\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒱</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒱</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{V}}=\{1,2,\ldots,|{\mathcal{V}}|\}</annotation></semantics></math> and generates a sequence in an autoregressive manner. Specifically, in step <math id="S2.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>h</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h</annotation></semantics></math>, given a context sequence <math id="S2.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(a_{1},\ldots,a_{h-1})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(a_{1},\ldots,a_{h-1})</annotation></semantics></math>, an LLM generates the next token according to the conditional distribution, i.e., <math id="S2.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.m5" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="a_{h}\sim\pi(\cdot|a_{1},\ldots,a_{h-1})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>h</mi></msub><mo>∼</mo><mi>π</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a_{h}\sim\pi(\cdot|a_{1},\ldots,a_{h-1})</annotation></semantics></math>. This autoregressive generation procedure proceeds until a designated End-Of-Sentence (EOS) token is generated or a predetermined maximum length <math id="S2.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>H</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H</annotation></semantics></math> is reached. For analytical clarity, we assume uniform response lengths of exactly <math id="S2.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>H</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H</annotation></semantics></math>, with appropriate padding applied after the EOS token.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS1.SSS0.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">MDP Formulation of LLM.</h4>

<div id="S2.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In this paper, we take an reinforcement learning perspective of LLMs and formalize the language generation task as an MDP <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib35" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ramamurthy et al., 2023</a>)</cite>, defined by a tuple <math id="S2.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}=\langle\mathcal{S},{\mathcal{V}},r,P,\rho,H\rangle" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mo>,</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒱</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>r</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>P</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ρ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>H</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}=\langle\mathcal{S},{\mathcal{V}},r,P,\rho,H\rangle</annotation></semantics></math>. Here the state space <math id="S2.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{S}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{S}}</annotation></semantics></math> is the set of all finite-length strings formed by the concatenation of elements in <math id="S2.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{V}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒱</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{V}}</annotation></semantics></math> and the action space is the vocabulary set <math id="S2.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{V}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒱</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{V}}</annotation></semantics></math>. At the beginning of each trajectory, a prompt <math id="S2.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s_{1}=(x_{1},x_{2},\cdots,x_{m})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s_{1}=(x_{1},x_{2},\cdots,x_{m})</annotation></semantics></math> of length <math id="S2.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>m</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m</annotation></semantics></math> is sampled from the initial state distribution <math id="S2.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ρ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho</annotation></semantics></math>, with <math id="S2.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m\in\mathbb{N}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>ℕ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m\in\mathbb{N}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\forall i\in[m],x_{i}\in\mathcal{V}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">∀</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>m</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒱</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\forall i\in[m],x_{i}\in\mathcal{V}</annotation></semantics></math>. At each step <math id="S2.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h\in[H]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h\in[H]</annotation></semantics></math>, the LLM selects an action (or equivalently, a token) <math id="S2.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a_{h}\in\mathcal{V}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>h</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒱</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a_{h}\in\mathcal{V}</annotation></semantics></math> according to <math id="S2.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.m12" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\pi(\cdot|s_{h})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>π</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>h</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi(\cdot|s_{h})</annotation></semantics></math>. The environment then transits to the next state <math id="S2.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s_{h+1}=(x,a_{1},\cdots,a_{h})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>s</mi><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>h</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s_{h+1}=(x,a_{1},\cdots,a_{h})</annotation></semantics></math>, rewarding the LLM with <math id="S2.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r(s_{h},a_{h})\in[0,1]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>h</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>h</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r(s_{h},a_{h})\in[0,1]</annotation></semantics></math>. That is, the transition model <math id="S2.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P:\mathcal{S}\times\mathcal{V}\to\Delta(\mathcal{S})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒱</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P:\mathcal{S}\times\mathcal{V}\to\Delta(\mathcal{S})</annotation></semantics></math> is deterministic. <math id="S2.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P(s_{h+1}|s_{h},a_{h})=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mrow><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>h</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>h</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P(s_{h+1}|s_{h},a_{h})=1</annotation></semantics></math> if and only if <math id="S2.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s_{h+1}=s_{h}\oplus a_{h}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>s</mi><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>h</mi></msub><mo>⊕</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>h</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s_{h+1}=s_{h}\oplus a_{h}</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="S2.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\oplus" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>⊕</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\oplus</annotation></semantics></math> means concatenation. The trajectory ends after a total of <math id="S2.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>H</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H</annotation></semantics></math> steps. In the context of RL, we also call <math id="S2.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>π</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi</annotation></semantics></math> as a policy. Throughout this paper, the terms “policy” and “LLM” will be used interchangeably. We measure the quality of a policy <math id="S2.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.m21" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>π</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi</annotation></semantics></math> by its policy value <math id="S2.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.m22" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V^{\pi}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>V</mi><mi>π</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V^{\pi}</annotation></semantics></math> defined as <math id="S2.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.m23" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V^{\pi}=\mathbb{E}_{\tau\sim\pi}\left[\sum_{h=1}^{H}r(s_{h},a_{h})\right]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>V</mi><mi>π</mi></msup><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><mrow><mi>τ</mi><mo>∼</mo><mi>π</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><mrow><msubsup><mo lspace="0em">∑</mo><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>H</mi></msubsup><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>h</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>h</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V^{\pi}=\mathbb{E}_{\tau\sim\pi}\left[\sum_{h=1}^{H}r(s_{h},a_{h})\right]</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">2.2 </span>Next-token Prediction for Training LLMs</h3>

<div id="S2.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Next-token prediction <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib39" title="" class="ltx_ref">Vaswani, 2017</a>)</cite> serves as the fundamental training objective for LLMs, where the goal is to train an LLM to maximize the likelihood of predicting the next token given a sequence of preceding tokens. Formally, given the dataset <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="{\mathcal{D}}=\{(s^{i}_{1},a^{i}_{1:H})\}_{i=1}^{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mo>=</mo><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>s</mi><mn>1</mn><mi>i</mi></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>a</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mi>H</mi></mrow><mi>i</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>n</mi></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{D}}=\{(s^{i}_{1},a^{i}_{1:H})\}_{i=1}^{n}</annotation></semantics></math>, next-token prediction solves the following optimization problem.</p>
<table id="S2.EGx1" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S2.E1"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.E1.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\displaystyle\max_{\pi}\sum_{i=1}^{n}\sum_{h=1}^{H}\log\left(\pi\left(a^{i}_{h}|s^{i}_{h}\right)\right)." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><munder><mi>max</mi><mi>π</mi></munder><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>n</mi></munderover></mstyle><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>H</mi></munderover></mstyle><mi>log</mi><mrow><mo>(</mo><mi>π</mi><mrow><mo>(</mo><msubsup><mi>a</mi><mi>h</mi><mi>i</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><msubsup><mi>s</mi><mi>h</mi><mi>i</mi></msubsup><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\max_{\pi}\sum_{i=1}^{n}\sum_{h=1}^{H}\log\left(\pi\left(a^{i}_{h}|s^{i}_{h}\right)\right).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(1)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS2.p1.2" class="ltx_p">This objective is employed across multiple LLMs training stages: during pre-training <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib32" title="" class="ltx_ref">Radford et al., 2019</a>; <a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">Brown et al., 2020</a>)</cite> with massive general-purpose web datasets, and during supervised fine-tuning <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib29" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ouyang et al., 2022</a>)</cite> with smaller collections of high-quality human responses. Notably, next-token prediction can be viewed as an instance of the famous behavior cloning approach <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib31" title="" class="ltx_ref">Pomerleau, 1991</a>)</cite> from an imitation learning (IL) perspective <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib28" title="" class="ltx_ref">Osa et al., 2018</a>)</cite>, where the model mimics the expert’s action in states from demonstrations.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">2.3 </span>Reinforcement Learning from Human Feedback</h3>

<div id="S2.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">While next-token prediction is effective for teaching a model to imitate high-quality demonstrations, aligning its behavior with human values often requires a more direct form of feedback. Reinforcement Learning from Human Feedback (RLHF) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib29" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ouyang et al., 2022</a>)</cite> is the standard paradigm for this task, which refines an SFT model using a reward signal derived from human preferences. The process typically involves two subsequent stages: reward modeling and RL fine-tuning.</p>
</div>
<section id="S2.SS3.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Reward Modeling.</h4>

<div id="S2.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The first step is to train a separate reward model (RM), denoted <math id="S2.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R_{\phi}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>ϕ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R_{\phi}</annotation></semantics></math> with parameters <math id="S2.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ϕ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi</annotation></semantics></math>, to predict which responses humans would prefer. This requires a preference dataset, <math id="S2.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{D}_{\text{pref}}=\{(x,y_{w},y_{l})_{i}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mtext>pref</mtext></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>w</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>l</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{D}_{\text{pref}}=\{(x,y_{w},y_{l})_{i}\}</annotation></semantics></math>, where for a given prompt <math id="S2.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S2.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y_{w}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>w</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y_{w}</annotation></semantics></math> is a response preferred by a human annotator (the “winner") and <math id="S2.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y_{l}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>l</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y_{l}</annotation></semantics></math> is the dispreferred response (the “loser"). The RM is trained to assign a higher scalar score to the winning response. Following the Bradley-Terry (BT) model <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">Bradley and Terry, 1952</a>)</cite>, the probability that <math id="S2.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y_{w}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>w</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y_{w}</annotation></semantics></math> is preferred over <math id="S2.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y_{l}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>l</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y_{l}</annotation></semantics></math> is modeled as:</p>
<table id="S2.E2" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.E2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P(y_{w}\succ y_{l}|x)=\sigma(R_{\phi}(x,y_{w})-R_{\phi}(x,y_{l}))" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>w</mi></msub><mo>≻</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>l</mi></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>ϕ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>w</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>ϕ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>l</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P(y_{w}\succ y_{l}|x)=\sigma(R_{\phi}(x,y_{w})-R_{\phi}(x,y_{l}))</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(2)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p1.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S2.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>σ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma</annotation></semantics></math> is the sigmoid function. The RM’s parameters <math id="S2.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ϕ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi</annotation></semantics></math> are optimized by minimizing the negative log-likelihood of the observed preferences in the dataset:</p>
<table id="S2.E3" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.E3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}_{\text{RM}}(\phi)=-\mathbb{E}_{(x,y_{w},y_{l})\sim\mathcal{D}_{\text{pref}}}\left[\log\sigma(R_{\phi}(x,y_{w})-R_{\phi}(x,y_{l}))\right]" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mtext>RM</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ϕ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>w</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>l</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∼</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mtext>pref</mtext></msub></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>ϕ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>w</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>ϕ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>l</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}_{\text{RM}}(\phi)=-\mathbb{E}_{(x,y_{w},y_{l})\sim\mathcal{D}_{\text{pref}}}\left[\log\sigma(R_{\phi}(x,y_{w})-R_{\phi}(x,y_{l}))\right]</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(3)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS3.SSS0.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Reinforcement Learning Fine-Tuning.</h4>

<div id="S2.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Once the RM is trained, the SFT model, <math id="S2.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{\text{SFT}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>π</mi><mtext>SFT</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{\text{SFT}}</annotation></semantics></math>, is further optimized using reinforcement learning. The SFT model is treated as the initial policy, <math id="S2.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{\theta}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{\theta}</annotation></semantics></math>, which is then updated to maximize the expected reward assigned by the RM. To prevent the policy from deviating too far from the distribution learned during SFT and to mitigate “reward hacking” (i.e., finding adversarial ways to maximize the reward score), a KL-divergence penalty is added to the objective. The final optimization problem is:</p>
<table id="S2.E4" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.E4.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\max_{\theta}\mathbb{E}_{x\sim\mathcal{D},y\sim\pi_{\theta}(\cdot|x)}\left[R_{\phi}(x,y)-\beta\cdot D_{\mathrm{KL}}(\pi_{\theta}(\cdot|x)\parallel\pi_{\text{SFT}}(\cdot|x))\right]" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><munder><mi>max</mi><mi>θ</mi></munder><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>∼</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo>∼</mo><mi>π</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msub><mrow><mo>[</mo><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>ϕ</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>β</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><msub><mi>D</mi><mi>KL</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∥</mo><msub><mi>π</mi><mtext>SFT</mtext></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\max_{\theta}\mathbb{E}_{x\sim\mathcal{D},y\sim\pi_{\theta}(\cdot|x)}\left[R_{\phi}(x,y)-\beta\cdot D_{\mathrm{KL}}(\pi_{\theta}(\cdot|x)\parallel\pi_{\text{SFT}}(\cdot|x))\right]</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(4)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p1.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S2.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{D}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{D}</annotation></semantics></math> is a distribution of prompts, <math id="S2.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y</annotation></semantics></math> is the full response generated by the policy <math id="S2.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{\theta}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{\theta}</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="S2.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>β</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta</annotation></semantics></math> is a hyperparameter controlling the strength of the KL penalty.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS4" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">2.4 </span>Inverse Reinforcement Learning</h3>

<div id="S2.SS4.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.SS4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Inverse Reinforcement Learning (IRL) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib25" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ng and Russell, 2000</a>)</cite> is a fundamental class of imitation learning methods that addresses the inverse problem of RL. Instead of learning a policy from a known reward function, IRL seeks to recover a reward model from expert demonstrations, under the assumption that these demonstrations are optimal or near-optimal.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS4.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.SS4.p2.1" class="ltx_p">A prominent and theoretically grounded approach is Maximum Entropy (MaxEnt) IRL <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib50" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ziebart et al., 2008</a>)</cite>. It resolves the ambiguity that many reward functions can explain the same behavior by seeking a reward that rationalizes the expert demonstrations while being maximally non-committal about the behavior uncovered in the data. This principle leads to a minimax optimization problem:</p>
<table id="S2.EGx2" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S2.E5"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.E5.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\displaystyle\max_{r}\min_{\pi}\left(\mathbb{E}_{\tau\sim\pi^{\operatorname{E}}}\left[\sum_{h=1}^{H}r(s_{h},a_{h})\right]-\mathbb{E}_{\tau\sim\pi}\left[\sum_{h=1}^{H}r(s_{h},a_{h})+\alpha H(\pi(\cdot|s_{h}))\right]\right)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><munder><mi>max</mi><mi>r</mi></munder><munder><mi>min</mi><mi>π</mi></munder><mrow><mo>(</mo><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><mrow><mi>τ</mi><mo>∼</mo><msup><mi>π</mi><mi>E</mi></msup></mrow></msub><mrow><mo>[</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>H</mi></munderover></mstyle><mi>r</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>h</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>h</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>]</mo></mrow><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><mrow><mi>τ</mi><mo>∼</mo><mi>π</mi></mrow></msub><mrow><mo>[</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>H</mi></munderover></mstyle><mi>r</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>h</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>h</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>+</mo><mi>α</mi><mi>H</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>π</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>h</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>]</mo></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\max_{r}\min_{\pi}\left(\mathbb{E}_{\tau\sim\pi^{\operatorname{E}}}\left[\sum_{h=1}^{H}r(s_{h},a_{h})\right]-\mathbb{E}_{\tau\sim\pi}\left[\sum_{h=1}^{H}r(s_{h},a_{h})+\alpha H(\pi(\cdot|s_{h}))\right]\right)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(5)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS4.p2.2" class="ltx_p">Here, the expectation <math id="S2.SS4.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{E}_{\tau\sim\pi^{\operatorname{E}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><mrow><mi>τ</mi><mo>∼</mo><msup><mi>π</mi><mi>E</mi></msup></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{E}_{\tau\sim\pi^{\operatorname{E}}}</annotation></semantics></math> is taken over the trajectory distribution induced by the expert policy <math id="S2.SS4.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi^{\operatorname{E}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>π</mi><mi>E</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi^{\operatorname{E}}</annotation></semantics></math> (approximated by the dataset <math id="S2.SS4.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{D}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{D}</annotation></semantics></math>), while the expectation <math id="S2.SS4.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{E}_{\tau\sim\pi}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><mrow><mi>τ</mi><mo>∼</mo><mi>π</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{E}_{\tau\sim\pi}</annotation></semantics></math> is over the trajectory distribution generated by a learned policy <math id="S2.SS4.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>π</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi</annotation></semantics></math>. Besides, <math id="S2.SS4.p2.m6" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="H(\pi(\cdot|s_{h}))=\mathbb{E}_{a_{h}\sim\pi(\cdot|s_{h})}\left[-\log\pi(a_{h}|s_{h})\right]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>H</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>π</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>h</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><mrow><mi>a</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>h</mi></msub><mo>∼</mo><mi>π</mi><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>s</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>h</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msub><mrow><mo>[</mo><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><mi>log</mi><mi>π</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>h</mi></msub><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>h</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H(\pi(\cdot|s_{h}))=\mathbb{E}_{a_{h}\sim\pi(\cdot|s_{h})}\left[-\log\pi(a_{h}|s_{h})\right]</annotation></semantics></math> represents the entropy of <math id="S2.SS4.p2.m7" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\pi(\cdot|s_{h})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>π</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>h</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi(\cdot|s_{h})</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.SS4.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math> is the regularization coefficient. The objective seeks a reward function <math id="S2.SS4.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>r</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r</annotation></semantics></math> that maximizes the margin between the expert’s expected return and the entropy-regularized return of the optimal policy <math id="S2.SS4.p2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>π</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi</annotation></semantics></math> under the same reward <math id="S2.SS4.p2.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>r</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<section id="S2.SS4.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Connection to Reward Modeling in RLHF.</h4>

<div id="S2.SS4.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.SS4.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">It is insightful to view the MaxEnt IRL objective as a more general, distribution-level formulation of the principle underlying the BT model used in RLHF. The objective under the BT model in Eq.(<a href="#S2.E3" title="Equation 3 ‣ Reward Modeling. ‣ 2.3 Reinforcement Learning from Human Feedback ‣ 2 Preliminaries ‣ Generalist Reward Models: Found Inside Large Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>) learns a reward function by maximizing the score difference between a specific “winner” trajectory <math id="S2.SS4.SSS0.Px1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y_{w}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>w</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y_{w}</annotation></semantics></math> and a “loser” trajectory <math id="S2.SS4.SSS0.Px1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y_{l}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>l</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y_{l}</annotation></semantics></math>. This is fundamentally a <em id="S2.SS4.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">pairwise</em> or <em id="S2.SS4.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">sample-level</em> comparison.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS4.SSS0.Px1.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.SS4.SSS0.Px1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">The MaxEnt IRL objective in Eq. (<a href="#S2.E5" title="Equation 5 ‣ 2.4 Inverse Reinforcement Learning ‣ 2 Preliminaries ‣ Generalist Reward Models: Found Inside Large Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a>) can be seen as the <em id="S2.SS4.SSS0.Px1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">distribution-level</em> analogue. Instead of comparing two sampled trajectories, it compares the entire expert distribution (<math id="S2.SS4.SSS0.Px1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi^{\operatorname{E}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>π</mi><mi>E</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi^{\operatorname{E}}</annotation></semantics></math>) against the optimal competing policy <math id="S2.SS4.SSS0.Px1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>π</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi</annotation></semantics></math> that arises under the reward function <math id="S2.SS4.SSS0.Px1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>r</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r</annotation></semantics></math>. The ‘<math id="S2.SS4.SSS0.Px1.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\min_{\pi}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>min</mi><mi>π</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\min_{\pi}</annotation></semantics></math>‘ term actively seeks the strongest possible competitor policy, and the ‘<math id="S2.SS4.SSS0.Px1.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\max_{r}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>max</mi><mi>r</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\max_{r}</annotation></semantics></math>‘ term then adjusts the reward to create the largest possible performance gap.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS4.SSS0.Px1.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.SS4.SSS0.Px1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">In this light, the RLHF reward modeling process can be interpreted as a practical, computationally tractable instantiation of the IRL principle. Rather than solving the complex ‘<math id="S2.SS4.SSS0.Px1.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\min_{\pi}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>min</mi><mi>π</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\min_{\pi}</annotation></semantics></math>‘ optimization problem, RLHF simplifies it and directly maximizes the reward margin between the “winner” against the “loser” in the preference dataset. Therefore, the BT model is a specific case within the broader theoretical framework that IRL provides. Our work returns to this more general IRL formulation to derive a reward function directly, without the need for explicit pairwise comparisons.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S3" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">3 </span>Finding the Endogenous Reward Within the Language Model</h2>

<div id="S3.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In this section, we establish our central theoretical claim: a principled reward function can be directly recovered from the logits of any language model trained with standard next-token prediction.</p>
</div>
<section id="S3.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.1 </span>Reward Learning from Inverse Reinforcement Learning</h3>

<div id="S3.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">As discussed in our preliminaries, the standard practice of reward modeling in RLHF can be seen as a practical, simplified instance of a more fundamental paradigm: Inverse Reinforcement Learning (IRL). This connection suggests that IRL is the natural and most principled framework for learning a reward function from data. This begs the question: rather than relying on the pairwise comparison heuristics common in RLHF, can we apply a more foundational IRL approach to recover the optimal reward function that best explains an expert dataset, <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{D}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{D}</annotation></semantics></math>? The primary challenge that has historically hindered this direct approach is that most IRL methods are formulated for online settings, requiring computationally expensive interactions.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">A more direct and practical approach is to formulate the problem using an offline IRL method based on inverse soft Q-learning <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib10" title="" class="ltx_ref">Garg et al., 2021</a>)</cite>. This method seeks to find a Q-function that best explains the expert data in a static dataset <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\mathcal{D}=\{(s^{i}_{1},a^{i}_{1:H})\}_{i=1}^{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mo>=</mo><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>s</mi><mn>1</mn><mi>i</mi></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>a</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mi>H</mi></mrow><mi>i</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>n</mi></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{D}=\{(s^{i}_{1},a^{i}_{1:H})\}_{i=1}^{n}</annotation></semantics></math>. The objective is to solve the following optimization problem:</p>
<table id="S3.EGx3" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S3.E6"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\max_{Q}\frac{1}{n}\sum_{i=1}^{n}\sum_{h=1}^{H}\left[Q(s^{i}_{h},a^{i}_{h})-\alpha\log\bigg(\sum_{a_{h}\in{\mathcal{V}}}\exp\left(Q(s^{i}_{h},a_{h})\right)/\alpha\bigg)\right]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><munder><mi>max</mi><mi>Q</mi></munder><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>n</mi></mfrac></mstyle></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>n</mi></munderover></mstyle><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>H</mi></munderover></mstyle><mrow><mo>[</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>Q</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>s</mi><mi>h</mi><mi>i</mi></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>a</mi><mi>h</mi><mi>i</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="2.100em" minsize="2.100em">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>h</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒱</mi></mrow></munder></mstyle><mrow><mi>exp</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mi>Q</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>s</mi><mi>h</mi><mi>i</mi></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>h</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>/</mo><mi>α</mi></mrow><mo maxsize="2.100em" minsize="2.100em">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\max_{Q}\frac{1}{n}\sum_{i=1}^{n}\sum_{h=1}^{H}\left[Q(s^{i}_{h},a^{i}_{h})-\alpha\log\bigg(\sum_{a_{h}\in{\mathcal{V}}}\exp\left(Q(s^{i}_{h},a_{h})\right)/\alpha\bigg)\right]</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(6)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS1.p2.2" class="ltx_p">Once we find the optimal Q-function, <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q^{\star}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>Q</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q^{\star}</annotation></semantics></math>, that maximizes this objective, the corresponding ideal reward function, <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r^{\star}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>r</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r^{\star}</annotation></semantics></math>, can be recovered via the <em id="S3.SS1.p2.2.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">inverse soft Bellman operator</em> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib10" title="" class="ltx_ref">Garg et al., 2021</a>)</cite>:</p>
<table id="S3.EGx4" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S3.E7"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle r^{\star}(s_{h},a_{h}):=Q^{\star}(s_{h},a_{h})-\alpha\log\left(\sum_{a_{h+1}\in{\mathcal{V}}}\exp\left(Q^{\star}(s_{h+1},a_{h+1})\right)/\alpha\right)." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>r</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>h</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>h</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>Q</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>h</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>h</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><msub><mi>a</mi><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒱</mi></mrow></munder></mstyle><mrow><mi>exp</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>Q</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>/</mo><mi>α</mi></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle r^{\star}(s_{h},a_{h}):=Q^{\star}(s_{h},a_{h})-\alpha\log\left(\sum_{a_{h+1}\in{\mathcal{V}}}\exp\left(Q^{\star}(s_{h+1},a_{h+1})\right)/\alpha\right).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(7)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS1.p2.3" class="ltx_p">This provides a principled, offline method to find a reward function. The critical question is: what is the solution, <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q^{\star}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>Q</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q^{\star}</annotation></semantics></math>, to this problem, and can we find it without a complex, new optimization procedure?</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.2 </span>Next-Token Prediction Recovers the IRL Solution</h3>

<div id="S3.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We now demonstrate that the solution to <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q^{\star}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>Q</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q^{\star}</annotation></semantics></math> posed in Eq. (<a href="#S3.E6" title="Equation 6 ‣ 3.1 Reward Learning from Inverse Reinforcement Learning ‣ 3 Finding the Endogenous Reward Within the Language Model ‣ Generalist Reward Models: Found Inside Large Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6</span></a>) is not something that needs to be computed from scratch. Rather, it is already embodied by the logits of any language model trained with the standard next-token prediction objective.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">To see this, let’s first analyze what kind of solution the IRL objective seeks. By applying a simple log-of-exponent transformation, the objective in Eq. (<a href="#S3.E6" title="Equation 6 ‣ 3.1 Reward Learning from Inverse Reinforcement Learning ‣ 3 Finding the Endogenous Reward Within the Language Model ‣ Generalist Reward Models: Found Inside Large Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6</span></a>) can be expressed as a maximum likelihood problem:</p>
<table id="S3.EGx5" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S3.E8"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\max_{Q}\frac{1}{n}\sum_{i=1}^{n}\sum_{h=1}^{H}\log\left(\frac{\exp(Q(s^{i}_{h},a^{i}_{h})/\alpha)}{\sum_{a^{\prime}_{h}\in{\mathcal{V}}}\exp(Q(s^{i}_{h},a^{\prime}_{h})/\alpha)}\right)." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><munder><mi>max</mi><mi>Q</mi></munder><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>n</mi></mfrac></mstyle></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>n</mi></munderover></mstyle><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>H</mi></munderover></mstyle><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mrow><mi>exp</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>Q</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>s</mi><mi>h</mi><mi>i</mi></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>a</mi><mi>h</mi><mi>i</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>/</mo><mi>α</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mrow><msub><mo>∑</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>a</mi><mi>h</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒱</mi></mrow></msub><mrow><mi>exp</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>Q</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>s</mi><mi>h</mi><mi>i</mi></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>a</mi><mi>h</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>/</mo><mi>α</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\max_{Q}\frac{1}{n}\sum_{i=1}^{n}\sum_{h=1}^{H}\log\left(\frac{\exp(Q(s^{i}_{h},a^{i}_{h})/\alpha)}{\sum_{a^{\prime}_{h}\in{\mathcal{V}}}\exp(Q(s^{i}_{h},a^{\prime}_{h})/\alpha)}\right).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(8)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS2.p2.2" class="ltx_p">The term inside the logarithm can be regarded as a policy, <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\pi_{Q}(\cdot|s_{h})=\mathrm{softmax}(Q(s_{h},\cdot);\alpha)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>Q</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>h</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>softmax</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Q</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>h</mi></msub><mo rspace="0em">,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>;</mo><mi>α</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{Q}(\cdot|s_{h})=\mathrm{softmax}(Q(s_{h},\cdot);\alpha)</annotation></semantics></math>. Therefore, the optimization problem seeks the Q-function whose corresponding policy, <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{Q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>Q</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{Q}</annotation></semantics></math>, maximizes the likelihood of the expert demonstrations in dataset <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{D}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{D}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS2.p3.1" class="ltx_p">This is precisely the goal of training a language model by next-token prediction. During its training, a language model, <math id="S3.SS2.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widehat{\pi}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>π</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widehat{\pi}</annotation></semantics></math>, is optimized to maximize the log-likelihood of the data as shown in Eq.(<a href="#S2.E1" title="Equation 1 ‣ 2.2 Next-token Prediction for Training LLMs ‣ 2 Preliminaries ‣ Generalist Reward Models: Found Inside Large Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>). The resulting policy, <math id="S3.SS2.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widehat{\pi}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>π</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widehat{\pi}</annotation></semantics></math>, is by definition the maximum likelihood estimate of the data-generating distribution. This policy is parameterized as a softmax over the model’s logits, which we denote as <math id="S3.SS2.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widehat{f}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>f</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widehat{f}</annotation></semantics></math>:</p>
<table id="S3.E9" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E9.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\widehat{\pi}(\cdot|s_{h})=\mathrm{softmax}(\widehat{f}(s_{h},\cdot);\alpha)." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>π</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>h</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>softmax</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>f</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>h</mi></msub><mo rspace="0em">,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>;</mo><mi>α</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widehat{\pi}(\cdot|s_{h})=\mathrm{softmax}(\widehat{f}(s_{h},\cdot);\alpha).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(9)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS2.p3.2" class="ltx_p">Since the language model’s policy <math id="S3.SS2.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widehat{\pi}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>π</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widehat{\pi}</annotation></semantics></math> is the solution to the maximum likelihood problem, it directly follows that its underlying logits, <math id="S3.SS2.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widehat{f}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>f</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widehat{f}</annotation></semantics></math>, are a valid solution for the Q-function in the IRL optimization problem of Eq. (<a href="#S3.E6" title="Equation 6 ‣ 3.1 Reward Learning from Inverse Reinforcement Learning ‣ 3 Finding the Endogenous Reward Within the Language Model ‣ Generalist Reward Models: Found Inside Large Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6</span></a>). We formalize this direct connection as follows:</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmprop1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_prop">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmprop1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Proposition 1</span></span><span id="Thmprop1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmprop1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmprop1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmprop1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="Thmprop1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widehat{\pi}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>π</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widehat{\pi}</annotation></semantics></math> be a language model trained on a dataset <math id="Thmprop1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{D}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{D}</annotation></semantics></math> via next-token prediction, yielding a policy <math id="Thmprop1.p1.m3" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\widehat{\pi}(\cdot|s_{h})=\mathrm{softmax}(\widehat{f}(s_{h},\cdot);\alpha)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>π</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>h</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>softmax</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>f</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>h</mi></msub><mo rspace="0em">,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>;</mo><mi>α</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widehat{\pi}(\cdot|s_{h})=\mathrm{softmax}(\widehat{f}(s_{h},\cdot);\alpha)</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="Thmprop1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widehat{f}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>f</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widehat{f}</annotation></semantics></math> are the model’s logits. The logits function <math id="Thmprop1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widehat{f}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>f</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widehat{f}</annotation></semantics></math> is a solution to the principled offline IRL objective in Eq. (<a href="#S3.E6" title="Equation 6 ‣ 3.1 Reward Learning from Inverse Reinforcement Learning ‣ 3 Finding the Endogenous Reward Within the Language Model ‣ Generalist Reward Models: Found Inside Large Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6</span></a>).</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS2.p4.1" class="ltx_p">This proposition provides the theoretical cornerstone of our work. It reveals that the logits of a language model are not just arbitrary scores; they are a principled Q-function that implicitly represents the optimal reward function for the data the model was trained on. This discovery provides a unified view on generation and evaluation, where the model’s policy <math id="S3.SS2.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widehat{\pi}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>π</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widehat{\pi}</annotation></semantics></math> is responsible for generation, while its logits <math id="S3.SS2.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widehat{f}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>f</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widehat{f}</annotation></semantics></math> (acting as a Q-function) are responsible for evaluation.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.3 </span>The Endogenous Reward</h3>

<div id="S3.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The direct connection established in <a href="#Thmprop1" title="Proposition 1. ‣ 3.2 Next-Token Prediction Recovers the IRL Solution ‣ 3 Finding the Endogenous Reward Within the Language Model ‣ Generalist Reward Models: Found Inside Large Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Proposition</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> gives us a powerful, training-free method to obtain a reward model. Given any language model trained via next-token prediction, whether during pre-training or a fine-tuning stage, we can simply take its logits, <math id="S3.SS3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widehat{Q}=\widehat{f}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>Q</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo>=</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>f</mi><mo>^</mo></mover></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widehat{Q}=\widehat{f}</annotation></semantics></math>, and substitute them into the inverse soft Bellman operator from Eq. (<a href="#S3.E7" title="Equation 7 ‣ 3.1 Reward Learning from Inverse Reinforcement Learning ‣ 3 Finding the Endogenous Reward Within the Language Model ‣ Generalist Reward Models: Found Inside Large Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7</span></a>).</p>
<table id="S3.EGx6" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S3.E10"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E10.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\widehat{r}(s_{h},a_{h})=\widehat{Q}(s_{h},a_{h})-\alpha\log\left(\sum_{a_{h+1}\in{\mathcal{V}}}\exp\left(\widehat{Q}(s_{h+1},a_{h+1})\right)/\alpha\right)." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>r</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>h</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>h</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>Q</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>h</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>h</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><msub><mi>a</mi><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒱</mi></mrow></munder></mstyle><mrow><mi>exp</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>Q</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>/</mo><mi>α</mi></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\widehat{r}(s_{h},a_{h})=\widehat{Q}(s_{h},a_{h})-\alpha\log\left(\sum_{a_{h+1}\in{\mathcal{V}}}\exp\left(\widehat{Q}(s_{h+1},a_{h+1})\right)/\alpha\right).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(10)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS3.p1.2" class="ltx_p">Let us define the value function <math id="S3.SS3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V_{\widehat{Q}}(s_{h}):=\alpha\log(\sum_{a_{h}\in{\mathcal{V}}}\exp(\widehat{Q}(s_{h},a_{h})/\alpha))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>V</mi><mover accent="true"><mi>Q</mi><mo>^</mo></mover></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>h</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mo lspace="0em">∑</mo><mrow><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>h</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒱</mi></mrow></msub><mrow><mi>exp</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>Q</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>h</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>h</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>/</mo><mi>α</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V_{\widehat{Q}}(s_{h}):=\alpha\log(\sum_{a_{h}\in{\mathcal{V}}}\exp(\widehat{Q}(s_{h},a_{h})/\alpha))</annotation></semantics></math>, which represents the expected future returns from state <math id="S3.SS3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>s</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s</annotation></semantics></math>. This results in the <span id="S3.SS3.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">endogenous reward</span>, written as:</p>
<table id="S3.EGx7" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S3.E11"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E11.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\widehat{r}(s_{h},a_{h})=\alpha\log\left(\widehat{\pi}(a_{h}|s_{h})\right)+V_{\widehat{Q}}(s_{h})-V_{\widehat{Q}}(s_{h+1})." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>r</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>h</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>h</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>π</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>h</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>h</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msub><mi>V</mi><mover accent="true"><mi>Q</mi><mo>^</mo></mover></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>h</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mi>V</mi><mover accent="true"><mi>Q</mi><mo>^</mo></mover></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\widehat{r}(s_{h},a_{h})=\alpha\log\left(\widehat{\pi}(a_{h}|s_{h})\right)+V_{\widehat{Q}}(s_{h})-V_{\widehat{Q}}(s_{h+1}).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(11)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS3.p1.3" class="ltx_p">We elaborate on this endogenous reward in the following three aspects.</p>
</div>
<section id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Reward Shaping.</h4>

<div id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">From Eq.(<a href="#S3.E11" title="Equation 11 ‣ 3.3 The Endogenous Reward ‣ 3 Finding the Endogenous Reward Within the Language Model ‣ Generalist Reward Models: Found Inside Large Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">11</span></a>), careful readers may notice that the endogenous reward <math id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widehat{r}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>r</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widehat{r}</annotation></semantics></math> can be viewed as a shaped reward <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib26" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ng et al., 1999</a>)</cite> of <math id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widetilde{r}(s_{h},a_{h}):=\log(\widehat{\pi}(a_{h}|s_{h}))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>r</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>h</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>h</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>π</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>h</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>h</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widetilde{r}(s_{h},a_{h}):=\log(\widehat{\pi}(a_{h}|s_{h}))</annotation></semantics></math> represented by the language model’s log-probability, with the potential function <math id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V_{\widehat{Q}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>V</mi><mover accent="true"><mi>Q</mi><mo>^</mo></mover></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V_{\widehat{Q}}</annotation></semantics></math>. According to the theory on reward shaping <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib26" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ng et al., 1999</a>)</cite>, <math id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widehat{r}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>r</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widehat{r}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widetilde{r}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>r</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widetilde{r}</annotation></semantics></math> induce the same optimal policy and value function.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Outcome Reward.</h4>

<div id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">For a complete response <math id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau=(s_{1},a_{1},\ldots,s_{H},a_{H})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>τ</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>H</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>H</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau=(s_{1},a_{1},\ldots,s_{H},a_{H})</annotation></semantics></math>, the resultant outcome reward can be calculated as</p>
<table id="S3.EGx8" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S3.E12"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E12.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\widehat{r}(\tau)=\sum_{h=1}^{H}\widehat{r}(s_{h},a_{h})=\alpha\sum_{h=1}^{H}\log\left(\widehat{\pi}(a_{h}|s_{h})\right)+V_{Q}(s_{1})=\alpha\log(\widehat{\pi}(\tau|s_{1}))+V_{\widehat{Q}}(s_{1})." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>r</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>τ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>H</mi></munderover></mstyle><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>r</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>h</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>h</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>H</mi></munderover></mstyle><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>π</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>h</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>h</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>Q</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>π</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>τ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msub><mi>V</mi><mover accent="true"><mi>Q</mi><mo>^</mo></mover></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\widehat{r}(\tau)=\sum_{h=1}^{H}\widehat{r}(s_{h},a_{h})=\alpha\sum_{h=1}^{H}\log\left(\widehat{\pi}(a_{h}|s_{h})\right)+V_{Q}(s_{1})=\alpha\log(\widehat{\pi}(\tau|s_{1}))+V_{\widehat{Q}}(s_{1}).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(12)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p1.2" class="ltx_p">The second equation follows the telescoping argument with the boundary condition <math id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widehat{Q}(s_{H+1},\cdot)\equiv 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>Q</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo rspace="0em">,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≡</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widehat{Q}(s_{H+1},\cdot)\equiv 0</annotation></semantics></math>. <math id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widehat{\pi}(\tau|s_{1}):=\prod_{h=1}^{H}\widehat{\pi}(a_{h}|s_{h})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>π</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>τ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">:=</mo><mrow><msubsup><mo>∏</mo><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>H</mi></msubsup><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>π</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>h</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>h</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widehat{\pi}(\tau|s_{1}):=\prod_{h=1}^{H}\widehat{\pi}(a_{h}|s_{h})</annotation></semantics></math> represents the probability of the complete response <math id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>τ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau</annotation></semantics></math> under <math id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widehat{\pi}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>π</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widehat{\pi}</annotation></semantics></math>. We can observe that the outcome reward equals the sum of log probabilities across the trajectory, up to the term <math id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V_{Q}(s_{1})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>Q</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V_{Q}(s_{1})</annotation></semantics></math> only depending on the initial state (i.e., prompt). Intuitively, if a response occurs frequently in the training data (e.g., massive pre-training data), then the trained LLM will generate this response with a higher likelihood, which also means the reward value of this response is higher.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px3" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Exisiting Generative Reward Models as Special Cases.</h4>

<div id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px3.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Eq.(<a href="#S3.E12" title="Equation 12 ‣ Outcome Reward. ‣ 3.3 The Endogenous Reward ‣ 3 Finding the Endogenous Reward Within the Language Model ‣ Generalist Reward Models: Found Inside Large Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">12</span></a>) introduces a general formulation that exploits a LLM’s probability for reward construction, with existing generative reward models <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib23" title="" class="ltx_ref">Mahan et al., 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib46" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhang et al., 2024</a>)</cite> emerging as particular instances of this framework. For instance, for a question-answer pair <math id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\textbf{x},\textbf{y})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_bold">x</mtext><mo>,</mo><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_bold">y</mtext><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\textbf{x},\textbf{y})</annotation></semantics></math>, the generative verifier in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib46" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhang et al., 2024</a>)</cite> uses a prompt <math id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\textbf{P}=\text{`Is the answer correct?'}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_bold">P</mtext><mo>=</mo><mtext>‘Is the answer correct?’</mtext></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\textbf{P}=\text{`Is the answer correct?'}</annotation></semantics></math>, and represents the reward as the probability of a single token ‘Yes’ under the context and the prompt, i.e., <math id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widehat{\pi}(\text{Yes}|\textbf{x},\textbf{y},\textbf{P})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>π</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mtext>Yes</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mrow><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_bold">x</mtext><mo>,</mo><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_bold">y</mtext><mo>,</mo><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_bold">P</mtext></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widehat{\pi}(\text{Yes}|\textbf{x},\textbf{y},\textbf{P})</annotation></semantics></math>. This corresponds to our method when setting <math id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s_{1}=(\textbf{x},\textbf{y},\textbf{P})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_bold">x</mtext><mo>,</mo><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_bold">y</mtext><mo>,</mo><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_bold">P</mtext><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s_{1}=(\textbf{x},\textbf{y},\textbf{P})</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px3.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a_{1}=\text{`Yes'}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>a</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mtext>‘Yes’</mtext></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a_{1}=\text{`Yes'}</annotation></semantics></math>, resulting the reward <math id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px3.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widehat{r}(\tau)=\alpha\log(\widehat{\pi}(\text{Yes}|\textbf{x},\textbf{y},\textbf{P}))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>r</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>τ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>π</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mtext>Yes</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mrow><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_bold">x</mtext><mo>,</mo><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_bold">y</mtext><mo>,</mo><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_bold">P</mtext></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widehat{r}(\tau)=\alpha\log(\widehat{\pi}(\text{Yes}|\textbf{x},\textbf{y},\textbf{P}))</annotation></semantics></math> consistent with theirs. Similar arguments can also be made for the generative reward model approach in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib23" title="" class="ltx_ref">Mahan et al., 2024</a>)</cite> designed for pair-wise comparisons. Based on this connection, the established <a href="#Thmprop1" title="Proposition 1. ‣ 3.2 Next-Token Prediction Recovers the IRL Solution ‣ 3 Finding the Endogenous Reward Within the Language Model ‣ Generalist Reward Models: Found Inside Large Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Proposition</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> can also provide a theoretical justification for these approaches.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S4" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">4 </span>Theoretical Justifications for the Endogenous Reward</h2>

<div id="S4.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In this part, we provide theoretical justifications for the endogenous reward by analyzing both the reward error and the induced policy error.</p>
</div>
<section id="S4.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.1 </span>Error Analysis of the Endogenous Reward.</h3>

<div id="S4.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In the sequel, we analyze the performance of the endogenous reward. Specifically, following maximum entropy inverse reinforcement learning <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib50" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ziebart et al., 2008</a>)</cite>, we consider that the expert policy <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi^{\operatorname{E}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>π</mi><mi>E</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi^{\operatorname{E}}</annotation></semantics></math> is the entropy-regularized optimal policy regarding the unknown true reward <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r^{\star}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>r</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r^{\star}</annotation></semantics></math>. According to the theory on regularized MDPs <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib11" title="" class="ltx_ref">Geist et al., 2019</a>)</cite>, it holds that</p>
<table id="S4.EGx9" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S4.E13"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.E13.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle r^{\star}(s_{h},a_{h})=\alpha\log\left(\pi^{\operatorname{E}}(a_{h}|s_{h})\right)+V_{Q^{\star}_{r^{\star}}}(s_{h})-V_{Q^{\star}_{r^{\star}}}(s_{h+1})." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>r</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>h</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>h</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>π</mi><mi>E</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>h</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>h</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msub><mi>V</mi><msubsup><mi>Q</mi><msup><mi>r</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msup><mo>⋆</mo></msubsup></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>h</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mi>V</mi><msubsup><mi>Q</mi><msup><mi>r</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msup><mo>⋆</mo></msubsup></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle r^{\star}(s_{h},a_{h})=\alpha\log\left(\pi^{\operatorname{E}}(a_{h}|s_{h})\right)+V_{Q^{\star}_{r^{\star}}}(s_{h})-V_{Q^{\star}_{r^{\star}}}(s_{h+1}).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(13)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS1.p1.2" class="ltx_p">Here <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q^{\star}_{r^{\star}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>Q</mi><msup><mi>r</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msup><mo>⋆</mo></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q^{\star}_{r^{\star}}</annotation></semantics></math> is the entropy-regularized optimal Q-value function with the true reward <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r^{\star}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>r</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r^{\star}</annotation></semantics></math>. First, due to the reward ambiguity issue that multiple reward functions can derive the same expert policy <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib26" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ng et al., 1999</a>)</cite>, it is impossible to approximate the true reward <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r^{\star}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>r</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r^{\star}</annotation></semantics></math> even with the full knowledge of <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi^{\operatorname{E}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>π</mi><mi>E</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi^{\operatorname{E}}</annotation></semantics></math>. Therefore, we cannot draw any theoretical claim on the absolute error <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|\widehat{r}(s_{h},a_{h})-r^{\star}(s_{h},a_{h})|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>r</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>h</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>h</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msup><mi>r</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>h</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>h</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|\widehat{r}(s_{h},a_{h})-r^{\star}(s_{h},a_{h})|</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Nevertheless, recall one primary goal of building reward models is to perform pair-wise comparisons <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib29" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ouyang et al., 2022</a>; <a href="#bib.bib2" title="" class="ltx_ref">Bai et al., 2022a</a>; <a href="#bib.bib24" title="" class="ltx_ref">Malik et al., 2025</a>)</cite>. Therefore, instead of analyzing the absolute error <math id="S4.SS1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|\widehat{r}-r^{\star}|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>r</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo>−</mo><msup><mi>r</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|\widehat{r}-r^{\star}|</annotation></semantics></math>, we turn to analyze the performance when leveraging reward models to perform comparisons for two responses.
Formally, the task is to leverage the reward model to evaluate the preference over a pair of responses <math id="S4.SS1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\tau,\tau^{\prime})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>τ</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>τ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\tau,\tau^{\prime})</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="S4.SS1.p2.m3" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\tau=(s_{1},a_{1:H}),\tau^{\prime}=(s_{1},a^{\prime}_{1:H})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>τ</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mi>H</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>τ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>a</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mi>H</mi></mrow><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau=(s_{1},a_{1:H}),\tau^{\prime}=(s_{1},a^{\prime}_{1:H})</annotation></semantics></math>. Following the Bradley-Terry model <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">Bradley and Terry, 1952</a>)</cite>, the preference distribution induced by reward <math id="S4.SS1.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>r</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r</annotation></semantics></math> can be formulated as <math id="S4.SS1.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{P}}_{r}(\tau\succ\tau^{\prime}|\tau,\tau^{\prime})=\sigma(r(\tau)-r(\tau^{\prime}))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>ℙ</mi><mi>r</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>τ</mi><mo>≻</mo><msup><mi>τ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mrow><mi>τ</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>τ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>τ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>τ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{P}}_{r}(\tau\succ\tau^{\prime}|\tau,\tau^{\prime})=\sigma(r(\tau)-r(\tau^{\prime}))</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="S4.SS1.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma(x)=1/(1+\exp(-x))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>exp</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma(x)=1/(1+\exp(-x))</annotation></semantics></math> is the sigmoid function. The theoretical goal is to analyze the divergence between the true preference distribution <math id="S4.SS1.p2.m7" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="{\mathbb{P}}_{r^{\star}}(\cdot|\tau,\tau^{\prime})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>ℙ</mi><msup><mi>r</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msup></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>τ</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>τ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{P}}_{r^{\star}}(\cdot|\tau,\tau^{\prime})</annotation></semantics></math> and the inferred one <math id="S4.SS1.p2.m8" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="{\mathbb{P}}_{\widehat{r}}(\cdot|\tau,\tau^{\prime})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>ℙ</mi><mover accent="true"><mi>r</mi><mo>^</mo></mover></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>τ</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>τ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{P}}_{\widehat{r}}(\cdot|\tau,\tau^{\prime})</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmthm1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_thm">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmthm1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 1</span></span><span id="Thmthm1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmthm1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmthm1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmthm1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">In token-level MDPs with the unknown true reward <math id="Thmthm1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r^{\star}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>r</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r^{\star}</annotation></semantics></math>, suppose that the expert policy <math id="Thmthm1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi^{\operatorname{E}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>π</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">E</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi^{\operatorname{E}}</annotation></semantics></math> is the entropy-regularized optimal policy. Consider that <math id="Thmthm1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widehat{\pi}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>π</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widehat{\pi}</annotation></semantics></math> is the policy trained by next-token prediction and <math id="Thmthm1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widehat{r}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>r</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widehat{r}</annotation></semantics></math> is the endogenous reward defined in Eq.(<a href="#S3.E10" title="Equation 10 ‣ 3.3 The Endogenous Reward ‣ 3 Finding the Endogenous Reward Within the Language Model ‣ Generalist Reward Models: Found Inside Large Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">10</span></a>). For any pair of responses <math id="Thmthm1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\tau,\tau^{\prime})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>τ</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>τ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\tau,\tau^{\prime})</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="Thmthm1.p1.m6" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\tau=(s_{1},a_{1:H}),\tau^{\prime}=(s_{1},a^{\prime}_{1:H})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>τ</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mi>H</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>τ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>a</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mi>H</mi></mrow><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau=(s_{1},a_{1:H}),\tau^{\prime}=(s_{1},a^{\prime}_{1:H})</annotation></semantics></math>, we have that</span></p>
<table id="S4.EGx10" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S4.Ex1"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.Ex1.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\displaystyle D_{\mathrm{TV}}\left({\mathbb{P}}_{r^{\star}}(\cdot|\tau,\tau^{\prime}),{\mathbb{P}}_{\widehat{r}}(\cdot|\tau,\tau^{\prime})\right)\leq\frac{\alpha H}{2}\varepsilon_{\pi}." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>D</mi><mi>TV</mi></msub><mrow><mo>(</mo><msub><mi>ℙ</mi><msup><mi>r</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msup></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>τ</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>τ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>ℙ</mi><mover accent="true"><mi>r</mi><mo>^</mo></mover></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>τ</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>τ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>H</mi></mrow><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mstyle><msub><mi>ε</mi><mi>π</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle D_{\mathrm{TV}}\left({\mathbb{P}}_{r^{\star}}(\cdot|\tau,\tau^{\prime}),{\mathbb{P}}_{\widehat{r}}(\cdot|\tau,\tau^{\prime})\right)\leq\frac{\alpha H}{2}\varepsilon_{\pi}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="Thmthm1.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmthm1.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Here <math id="Thmthm1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D_{\mathrm{TV}}(p,q)=(1/2)\sum_{x\in{\mathcal{X}}}|p(x)-q(x)|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>D</mi><mi>TV</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>p</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>q</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><msub><mo rspace="0em">∑</mo><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi></mrow></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D_{\mathrm{TV}}(p,q)=(1/2)\sum_{x\in{\mathcal{X}}}|p(x)-q(x)|</annotation></semantics></math> is the total variation distance between two distributions <math id="Thmthm1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p,q\in\Delta({\mathcal{X}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p,q\in\Delta({\mathcal{X}})</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="Thmthm1.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varepsilon_{\pi}:=\max_{h\in[H]}\max_{(s_{h},a_{h})}|\log(\pi^{\operatorname{E}}(a_{h}|s_{h}))-\log(\widehat{\pi}(a_{h}|s_{h}))|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>ε</mi><mi>π</mi></msub><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>max</mi><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msub><mi>max</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>h</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>h</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>π</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">E</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>h</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>h</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>π</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>h</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>h</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varepsilon_{\pi}:=\max_{h\in[H]}\max_{(s_{h},a_{h})}|\log(\pi^{\operatorname{E}}(a_{h}|s_{h}))-\log(\widehat{\pi}(a_{h}|s_{h}))|</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS1.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS1.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><a href="#Thmthm1" title="Theorem 1. ‣ 4.1 Error Analysis of the Endogenous Reward. ‣ 4 Theoretical Justifications for the Endogenous Reward ‣ Generalist Reward Models: Found Inside Large Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Theorem</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> indicates that if the LLM <math id="S4.SS1.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widehat{\pi}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>π</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widehat{\pi}</annotation></semantics></math> used to extract rewards is closed to the underlying expert <math id="S4.SS1.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi^{\operatorname{E}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>π</mi><mi>E</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi^{\operatorname{E}}</annotation></semantics></math> in log-probabilities across the responses, then the preference distribution induced by <math id="S4.SS1.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widehat{r}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>r</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widehat{r}</annotation></semantics></math> is closed to that induced by <math id="S4.SS1.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r^{\star}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>r</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r^{\star}</annotation></semantics></math>. This suggests that the proposed endogenous reward extracted from LLMs can provably inherit their performance.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.2 </span>Error Analysis of the LLM Finetuned by the Endogenous Reward</h3>

<div id="S4.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The ultimate goal of eliciting the reward function is to use it for training a new, improved policy via reinforcement learning. Here we analyze the performance of the newly learned policy and make a comparison with the base policy.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Specifically, we analyze two approaches. The first approach directly applies next-token prediction (i.e., behavior cloning) on demonstrations, yielding the base policy <math id="S4.SS2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widehat{\pi}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>π</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widehat{\pi}</annotation></semantics></math>. Another approach first builds the endogenous reward based on Eq.(<a href="#S3.E10" title="Equation 10 ‣ 3.3 The Endogenous Reward ‣ 3 Finding the Endogenous Reward Within the Language Model ‣ Generalist Reward Models: Found Inside Large Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">10</span></a>) from <math id="S4.SS2.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widehat{\pi}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>π</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widehat{\pi}</annotation></semantics></math>, and then applies reinforcement learning using <math id="S4.SS2.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widehat{r}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>r</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widehat{r}</annotation></semantics></math>, yielding the new policy <math id="S4.SS2.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi^{\operatorname{RL}}=\mathop{\rm argmax}_{\pi}V^{\pi}_{\widehat{r}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>π</mi><mi>RL</mi></msup><mo rspace="0.1389em">=</mo><mrow><msub><mo lspace="0.1389em" rspace="0.167em">argmax</mo><mi>π</mi></msub><msubsup><mi>V</mi><mover accent="true"><mi>r</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>π</mi></msubsup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi^{\operatorname{RL}}=\mathop{\rm argmax}_{\pi}V^{\pi}_{\widehat{r}}</annotation></semantics></math>. Here, we ignore any optimization error in solving the optimal policy. The theoretical goal is to analyze and compare the sub-optimality of <math id="S4.SS2.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi^{\operatorname{RL}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>π</mi><mi>RL</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi^{\operatorname{RL}}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.SS2.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widehat{\pi}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>π</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widehat{\pi}</annotation></semantics></math> under the true reward <math id="S4.SS2.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r^{\star}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>r</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r^{\star}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmthm2" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_thm">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmthm2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 2</span></span><span id="Thmthm2.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmthm2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmthm2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmthm2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">In token-level MDPs with the unknown true reward <math id="Thmthm2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r^{\star}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>r</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r^{\star}</annotation></semantics></math>, suppose that the expert policy <math id="Thmthm2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi^{\operatorname{E}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>π</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">E</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi^{\operatorname{E}}</annotation></semantics></math> is the entropy-regularized optimal policy. Consider that <math id="Thmthm2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widehat{\pi}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>π</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widehat{\pi}</annotation></semantics></math> is the policy trained by next-token prediction and <math id="Thmthm2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widehat{r}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>r</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widehat{r}</annotation></semantics></math> is the extracted reward defined in Eq.(<a href="#S3.E10" title="Equation 10 ‣ 3.3 The Endogenous Reward ‣ 3 Finding the Endogenous Reward Within the Language Model ‣ Generalist Reward Models: Found Inside Large Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">10</span></a>). Apply reinforcement learning using the extracted reward <math id="Thmthm2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widehat{r}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>r</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widehat{r}</annotation></semantics></math> to learn a new policy <math id="Thmthm2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi^{\operatorname{RL}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>π</mi><mi>RL</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi^{\operatorname{RL}}</annotation></semantics></math>, i.e., <math id="Thmthm2.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi^{\operatorname{RL}}=\mathop{\rm argmax}_{\pi}V^{\pi}_{\widehat{r}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>π</mi><mi>RL</mi></msup><mo rspace="0.1389em">=</mo><mrow><msub><mo lspace="0.1389em" rspace="0.167em">argmax</mo><mi>π</mi></msub><msubsup><mi>V</mi><mover accent="true"><mi>r</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>π</mi></msubsup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi^{\operatorname{RL}}=\mathop{\rm argmax}_{\pi}V^{\pi}_{\widehat{r}}</annotation></semantics></math>. Then we have that</span></p>
<table id="S4.EGx11" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S4.Ex2"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.Ex2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle V^{\pi^{\operatorname{E}}}_{r^{\star}}-V^{\widehat{\pi}}_{r^{\star}}\precsim H^{2}\varepsilon_{\pi}\qquad\qquad\textrm{and}\qquad\qquad V^{\pi^{\operatorname{E}}}_{r^{\star}}-V^{\pi^{\operatorname{RL}}}_{r^{\star}}\precsim H\varepsilon_{\pi}." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>V</mi><msup><mi>r</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msup><msup><mi>π</mi><mi>E</mi></msup></msubsup><mo>−</mo><msubsup><mi>V</mi><msup><mi>r</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msup><mover accent="true"><mi>π</mi><mo>^</mo></mover></msubsup></mrow><mo>≾</mo><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>ε</mi><mi>π</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><mspace style="width:4em;" width="4em"></mspace><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic">and</mtext><mspace style="width:4em;" width="4em"></mspace><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>V</mi><msup><mi>r</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msup><msup><mi>π</mi><mi>E</mi></msup></msubsup><mo>−</mo><msubsup><mi>V</mi><msup><mi>r</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msup><msup><mi>π</mi><mi>RL</mi></msup></msubsup></mrow><mo>≾</mo><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>ε</mi><mi>π</mi></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle V^{\pi^{\operatorname{E}}}_{r^{\star}}-V^{\widehat{\pi}}_{r^{\star}}\precsim H^{2}\varepsilon_{\pi}\qquad\qquad\textrm{and}\qquad\qquad V^{\pi^{\operatorname{E}}}_{r^{\star}}-V^{\pi^{\operatorname{RL}}}_{r^{\star}}\precsim H\varepsilon_{\pi}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="Thmthm2.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmthm2.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Here <math id="Thmthm2.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V^{\pi}_{r}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>V</mi><mi>r</mi><mi>π</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V^{\pi}_{r}</annotation></semantics></math> represents the value of <math id="Thmthm2.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>π</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi</annotation></semantics></math> under <math id="Thmthm2.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>r</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="Thmthm2.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varepsilon_{\pi}:=\max_{h\in[H]}\max_{(s_{h},a_{h})}|\log(\pi^{\operatorname{E}}(a_{h}|s_{h}))-\log(\widehat{\pi}(a_{h}|s_{h}))|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>ε</mi><mi>π</mi></msub><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>max</mi><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msub><mi>max</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>h</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>h</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>π</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">E</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>h</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>h</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>π</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>h</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>h</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varepsilon_{\pi}:=\max_{h\in[H]}\max_{(s_{h},a_{h})}|\log(\pi^{\operatorname{E}}(a_{h}|s_{h}))-\log(\widehat{\pi}(a_{h}|s_{h}))|</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS2.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS2.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><a href="#Thmthm2" title="Theorem 2. ‣ 4.2 Error Analysis of the LLM Finetuned by the Endogenous Reward ‣ 4 Theoretical Justifications for the Endogenous Reward ‣ Generalist Reward Models: Found Inside Large Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Theorem</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a> indicates that <math id="S4.SS2.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi^{\operatorname{RL}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>π</mi><mi>RL</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi^{\operatorname{RL}}</annotation></semantics></math> achieves a linear dependence on the response length <math id="S4.SS2.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>H</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H</annotation></semantics></math> in the sub-optimality bound, while <math id="S4.SS2.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widehat{\pi}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>π</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widehat{\pi}</annotation></semantics></math> suffers a quadractic dependence. This quadratic dependence reflects the compounding errors issue in imitation learning <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib36" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ross and Bagnell, 2010</a>; <a href="#bib.bib42" title="" class="ltx_ref">Xu et al., 2021</a>)</cite>, where the single-step error <math id="S4.SS2.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varepsilon_{\pi}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ε</mi><mi>π</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varepsilon_{\pi}</annotation></semantics></math> accumulates along with the trajectory. The superior linear dependence achieved by <math id="S4.SS2.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi^{\operatorname{RL}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>π</mi><mi>RL</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi^{\operatorname{RL}}</annotation></semantics></math> reveals a fundamental advantage of inverse reinforcement learning. Instead of directly imitating expert actions, recovering the underlying reward function and performing RL with this reward eliminates the compounding error problem. As a technical remark, although this paper focuses on the finite-horizon setup, <a href="#Thmthm2" title="Theorem 2. ‣ 4.2 Error Analysis of the LLM Finetuned by the Endogenous Reward ‣ 4 Theoretical Justifications for the Endogenous Reward ‣ Generalist Reward Models: Found Inside Large Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Theorem</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a> can be extended to the infinite-horizon setting through the analysis developed in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib42" title="" class="ltx_ref">Xu et al., 2021</a>)</cite>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.3 </span>Ineffectiveness of Iterative Improvement</h3>

<div id="S4.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">A natural question is whether this self-improvement process can be repeated: can we take the improved policy <math id="S4.SS3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi^{\operatorname{RL}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>π</mi><mi>RL</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi^{\operatorname{RL}}</annotation></semantics></math>, extract its own endogenous reward, and perform another RL step to gain further improvements? The answer is no. By construction, the policy <math id="S4.SS3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi^{\operatorname{RL}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>π</mi><mi>RL</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi^{\operatorname{RL}}</annotation></semantics></math> is the optimal policy for the reward <math id="S4.SS3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widehat{r}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>r</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widehat{r}</annotation></semantics></math>. Consequently, the endogenous reward extracted from <math id="S4.SS3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi^{\operatorname{RL}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>π</mi><mi>RL</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi^{\operatorname{RL}}</annotation></semantics></math> is precisely the one for which <math id="S4.SS3.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi^{\operatorname{RL}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>π</mi><mi>RL</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi^{\operatorname{RL}}</annotation></semantics></math> is already optimal. Applying another RL step would therefore yield the same policy, causing the process to converge immediately.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S5" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">5 </span>Experiments</h2>

<div id="S5.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In this section, we conduct experiments to empirically validate our theoretical claims<span id="footnote1" class="ltx_note ltx_role_footnote"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">1</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">1</sup>
            <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_note">1</span>
            
            
            
            
            
            
            
          Due to time limitation, we report initial experimental results, and more results will be presented in future versions.</span></span></span>. Our evaluation is designed to answer three central research questions:</p>
<ul id="S5.I1" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="S5.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S5.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.I1.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Q1:</span> How does the training-free endogenous reward model (EndoRM) perform on common reward model benchmarks compared to both heuristic baselines and explicitly trained, state-of-the-art reward models?</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S5.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S5.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.I1.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Q2:</span> Does the endogenous reward have a strong instruction-following ability, functioning as a generalist reward model that can be prompted?</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S5.I1.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S5.I1.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.I1.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.I1.i3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Q3:</span> Can reinforcement learning with the endogenous reward yield a better policy, achieving self-improvement as predicted by our theory?</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div>
<section id="S5.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">5.1 </span>Experimental Setup</h3>

<section id="S5.SS1.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Datasets</h4>

<div id="S5.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We use <span id="S5.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">RM-Bench</span> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib22" title="" class="ltx_ref">Liu et al., 2025</a>)</cite> as a testbed to benchmark the general performance of different reward models (<span id="S5.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Q1</span>). To evaluate the instruction-following ability of EndoRM (<span id="S5.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Q2</span>), we use the <span id="S5.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Multifaceted-Bench</span> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib18" title="" class="ltx_ref">Lee et al., 2024b</a>)</cite>, which contains diverse user-specific preferences, and the <span id="S5.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Domain-Specific Preference (DSP)</span> dataset <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib6" title="" class="ltx_ref">Cheng et al., 2023</a>)</cite>, which has distinct preferences for different professional domains. Finally, to verify that RL with our EndoRM can lead to self-improvement (<span id="S5.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Q3</span>), we use <span id="S5.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">MATH-lighteval</span> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib15" title="" class="ltx_ref">Hendrycks et al., 2021</a>)</cite> as the training dataset.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S5.SS1.SSS0.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Evaluation Metrics</h4>

<div id="S5.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">For RM-Bench, Multifacted-Bench and DSP, we calculate the classification accuracy of the reward model for the two responses under a given prompt. For our method, we compute the total reward sum over all tokens for each response, as shown in <a href="#A3" title="Appendix C Outcome Reward Calculation ‣ Generalist Reward Models: Found Inside Large Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Appendix</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">C</span></a>. The response with the higher reward sum is classified as "chosen". For RL, we select five widely used math reasoning benchmarks including AIME 2024, AMC <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib21" title="" class="ltx_ref">Li et al., 2024b</a>)</cite>, Minerva <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib19" title="" class="ltx_ref">Lewkowycz et al., 2022</a>)</cite>, OlympiadBench <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib14" title="" class="ltx_ref">He et al., 2024</a>)</cite>, MATH-500 <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib15" title="" class="ltx_ref">Hendrycks et al., 2021</a>)</cite>. The temperatures for training and test are set to 1.0 and 0.6, respectively. For AIME 2024 and AMC, we report avg@32, and for the other three benchmarks, we report pass@1.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S5.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">5.2 </span>Reward Accuracy on Diverse Preference Pairs (Q1)</h3>

<div id="S5.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">To answer <span id="S5.SS2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Q1</span>, we evaluate reward models by using them to predict the chosen response in RM-Bench. Higher accuracy implies better quality of the reward. Since our method does not require training, we compare it against other training-free approaches: <span id="S5.SS2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Generative Verifier</span> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib46" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhang et al., 2024</a>)</cite>, <span id="S5.SS2.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">GenRM-Pairwise</span>, and <span id="S5.SS2.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">GenRM-Pointwise</span> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib23" title="" class="ltx_ref">Mahan et al., 2024</a>)</cite>. The prompt templates are in Appendix <a href="#A4" title="Appendix D Prompt Templates ‣ Generalist Reward Models: Found Inside Large Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">D</span></a>. All baselines and our EndoRM use <span id="S5.SS2.p1.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Qwen2.5-7B-Instruct</span> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib44" title="" class="ltx_ref">Yang et al., 2024</a>)</cite> as the base model for a fair comparison. We also include results from four top-performing, explicitly trained reward models for context.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">The results in <a href="#S5.T1" title="In 5.2 Reward Accuracy on Diverse Preference Pairs (Q1) ‣ 5 Experiments ‣ Generalist Reward Models: Found Inside Large Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Table</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> show that EndoRM not only substantially outperforms all training-free baselines using the same base model but also achieves a higher average score than the state-of-the-art explicitly trained reward models, supporting that the endogenous reward can be more effective than reward models that require expensive preference data curation and training.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS2.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.SS2.p3.1" class="ltx_p">We further present the results on the Multifaceted-Bench in <a href="#S5.F1" title="In 5.2 Reward Accuracy on Diverse Preference Pairs (Q1) ‣ 5 Experiments ‣ Generalist Reward Models: Found Inside Large Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Figure</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>, from which we can see that our EndoRM consistently outperforms all the baselines across five domains. Considering that there can be thousands of preferences in Multifaceted-Bench, this demonstrates that EndoRM achieves scalable robustness even as task complexity and preference diversity increase. This supports our core hypothesis that a powerful reward signal is already latent within the base model.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S5.T1" class="ltx_table">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table">Table 1: </span>Response classification accuracy by reward models on the RM-Bench. We bold highest score in each column.</figcaption>
