<section id="S5.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">5.3 </span>Verifying the Instruction-Following Ability (Q2)</h3>

<div id="S5.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">A key claim is that the endogenous reward is not static but can be prompted. To validate this, we use the DSP dataset, which contains four distinct domains. We create four domain-specific versions of the endogenous reward by simply providing the corresponding system prompt from the DSP paper as input. We then test the response classification accuracy of each domain-specific endogenous reward across all four test sets. To obtain the endogenous reward for each domain, the reward model will take the system prompts presented in Appendix A of <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib6" title="" class="ltx_ref">Cheng et al. (2023)</a></cite> as input. The results in Table <a href="#S5.T2" title="Table 2 ‣ 5.3 Verifying the Instruction-Following Ability (Q2) ‣ 5 Experiments ‣ Generalist Reward Models: Found Inside Large Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a> show a strong diagonal pattern: each EndoRM performs best on its own domain. For example, EndoRM-Academy achieves its highest accuracy (76.89%) on academic data. This confirms that the endogenous reward is not a fixed evaluator but a dynamic, promptable judge that inherits the powerful instruction-following capabilities of the base LLM.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S5.T2" class="ltx_table">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table">Table 2: </span>Response classification accuracy of EndoRM with cross-domain instructions on the DSP dataset.</figcaption>
