<section id="S5.SS4" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">5.4 </span>Self-Improvement via Reinforcement Learning (Q3)</h3>

<div id="S5.SS4.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.SS4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Finally, we test our central theoretical claim from Theorem <a href="#Thmthm2" title="Theorem 2. ‣ 4.2 Error Analysis of the LLM Finetuned by the Endogenous Reward ‣ 4 Theoretical Justifications for the Endogenous Reward ‣ Generalist Reward Models: Found Inside Large Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>: that RL with the endogenous reward can improve upon the base policy by mitigating compounding errors. We train the base model Qwen2.5-Math-7B via RL <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib37" title="" class="ltx_ref">Shao et al., 2024</a>)</cite> on the
MATH-lighteval dataset. The endogenous reward model is also Qwen2.5-Math-7B, whose parameters are fixed during policy learning. The maximum lengths of both prompts and responses are set to <math id="S5.SS4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1024" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>1024</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1024</annotation></semantics></math>, and the KL coefficient is set to <math id="S5.SS4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0.01" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0.01</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0.01</annotation></semantics></math>. The results in <a href="#S5.T3" title="In 5.4 Self-Improvement via Reinforcement Learning (Q3) ‣ 5 Experiments ‣ Generalist Reward Models: Found Inside Large Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Table</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a> show that the reinforcement learning fine-tuning with the endogenous reward helps the model consistently outperform the base model across all five benchmarks. We also present an example of the responses from models before and after RL in <a href="#A5" title="Appendix E Samples of Generated Responses ‣ Generalist Reward Models: Found Inside Large Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Appendix</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">E</span></a>, where we can see that for the same question, before optimization based on endogenous reward, the model failed to solve the problem and began to talk nonsense as the response progressed, even outputting Python code. In contrast, our method provided a clear and concise solution.</p>
</div>
