</section>
<section id="S6" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">6 </span>Related Work</h2>

<div id="S6.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S6.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Our work intersects with a burgeoning field of research aimed at reducing the dependency on human preference data for reward modeling.</p>
</div>
<div id="S6.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S6.p2.1" class="ltx_p">The most established alternative to human labeling is Reinforcement Learning from AI Feedback (RLAIF) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib3" title="" class="ltx_ref">Bai et al., 2022b</a>; <a href="#bib.bib30" title="" class="ltx_ref">Pang et al., 2023</a>; <a href="#bib.bib17" title="" class="ltx_ref">Lee et al., 2024a</a>)</cite>, often operationalized through the “LLM-as-a-judge” framework <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib48" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zheng et al., 2023</a>)</cite>. In this paradigm, a powerful, proprietary “teacher” model (e.g., GPT-4) is prompted to evaluate the outputs of a smaller “student” model. This evaluation can take several forms: generating a direct numerical score <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib48" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zheng et al., 2023</a>)</cite>, expressing a pairwise preference between two candidate responses <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib17" title="" class="ltx_ref">Lee et al., 2024a</a>)</cite>, or providing structured natural language critiques that are then used to guide alignment <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib3" title="" class="ltx_ref">Bai et al., 2022b</a>; <a href="#bib.bib30" title="" class="ltx_ref">Pang et al., 2023</a>)</cite>. A more recent line of work has focused on unifying the architecture of the policy and the reward model. Termed Generative Reward Models (GenRMs) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib23" title="" class="ltx_ref">Mahan et al., 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib46" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhang et al., 2024</a>)</cite>, these approaches frame reward modeling as a next-token prediction task. Instead of outputting a scalar score, the reward is defined as the probability of the model generating a specific token or sequence that signifies correctness or quality (e.g., the token “Yes” in response to a critique prompt). RLIF (reinforcement learning from internal feedback) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib47" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhao et al., 2025</a>)</cite> makes more direct use of internal representations termed self-certainty to form a reward signal. While these methods have proven effective and cost-efficient, they are fundamentally <em id="S6.p2.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">heuristic</em>. They rely on the unverified assumption that the teacher model’s judgment is a reliable proxy for human values and are susceptible to transferring the teacher’s stylistic biases to the student model.</p>
</div>
<div id="S6.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S6.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Another class of methods, led by Direct Preference Optimization (DPO) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib33" title="" class="ltx_ref">Rafailov et al., 2023</a>)</cite> and its successors like IPO <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib1" title="" class="ltx_ref">Azar et al., 2024</a>)</cite> and KTO <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib9" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ethayarajh et al., 2024</a>)</cite>, bypasses the explicit reward modeling stage altogether. DPO directly optimizes the language model on preference pairs by reformulating the RLHF objective as a simple classification loss. While DPO does not train an explicit RM, it learns an <em id="S6.p3.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">implicit</em> one. The optimal DPO policy is mathematically equivalent to the policy that would have been learned via RLHF with a specific reward function derived from the SFT model and the preference data. This implicit reward can be extracted from the DPO-trained model. However, like traditional RLHF, this entire process is still contingent on the availability of a preference dataset (generated by humans or AI) to drive the optimization.</p>
</div>
<div id="S6.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S6.p4.1" class="ltx_p">Our work introduces a fundamentally different approach. We posit that a reward function is not something that needs to be generated by an external judge (as RLHF) or implicitly optimized with preference pairs (as in DPO). Instead, we demonstrate that a principled reward function is an <span id="S6.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">intrinsic and latent property</span> of any model trained with next-token prediction. By establishing the theoretical equivalence between this training objective and offline inverse reinforcement learning, we provide a method to directly <em id="S6.p4.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">uncover</em> this reward function. This makes our approach training-free, independent of external models, and grounded in a formal theoretical framework, distinguishing it from all prior work in the field.</p>
</div>
<div id="S6.p5" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S6.p5.1" class="ltx_p">We also note that there is a growing body of works <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib40" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wulfmeier et al., 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib38" title="" class="ltx_ref">Sun et al., 2023</a>; <a href="#bib.bib20" title="" class="ltx_ref">Li et al., 2024a</a>)</cite> that leverage IRL to learn RMs in the literature of LLMs. In particular, they focus on the post-training stage and apply IRL methods to learn RMs from demonstrations (i.e., high-quality human responses). Our work differs from this research in two key aspects. First, rather than targeting specific downstream tasks, we aim to develop generalist reward models. Second, our method extracts rewards directly from pre-trained language models without requiring additional training on human responses, naturally inheriting the capabilities of LLMs already encoded during pre-training.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S7" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">7 </span>Discussion</h2>

<div id="S7.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S7.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The concept of an endogenous reward has profound implications for the field of LLMs alignment and development. We discuss some of the most significant consequences below.</p>
</div>
<section id="S7.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">7.1 </span>A Paradigm Shift in Model Alignment</h3>

<div id="S7.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S7.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The most immediate impact of our approach is the complete collapse of the traditional three-stage alignment process. <em id="S7.SS1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">The distinct reward modeling phase is rendered obsolete</em> before the reinforcement learning fine-tuning. This is a paradigm-shifting simplification. Any data previously collected for preference modeling can now be repurposed and integrated directly into the pre-training or SFT phase. By absorbing such data, the SFT model itself becomes more adept at evaluation, thereby strengthening its own endogenous reward mechanism and thus the model after reinforcement learning. In such a way, our approach reassembles the <em id="S7.SS1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">reinforcement pre-training</em> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib8" title="" class="ltx_ref">Dong et al., 2025</a>)</cite> idea but in a more principled way.</p>
</div>
<div id="S7.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S7.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">This streamlined workflow (without the training of a separate reward model) reduces the engineering complexity, computational cost, and time-to-deployment for aligned models.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S7.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">7.2 </span>Enhanced Personalization and Controllability</h3>

<div id="S7.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S7.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">An external reward model is trained on a static dataset of aggregated human preferences, making it a “one-size-fits-all” generalist judge. Personalizing its behavior for a specific user or enterprise context is infeasible without retraining.</p>
</div>
<div id="S7.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S7.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">The endogenous reward, by contrast, is dynamic and controllable at inference time. The evaluation criterion is not fixed but is defined by the natural language meta-question. This allows for unprecedented flexibility. A user could guide the model’s RL training towards their specific values by simply altering the instruction. This helps transform alignment from a static, post-hoc process into a dynamic, interactive dialogue with the model, enabling a new frontier of personalized AI. We have noted some recent efforts in this direction, e.g., <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib45" title="" class="ltx_ref">Yu et al., 2025</a>)</cite>, but the endogenous reward provides a training-free approach.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S7.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">7.3 </span>Reinforcement Model Distillation</h3>

<div id="S7.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S7.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Our framework introduces a novel and more principled approach to knowledge distillation. Knowledge distillation <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib49" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhou and Jiang, 2004</a>; <a href="#bib.bib16" title="" class="ltx_ref">Hinton, 2015</a>)</cite> is often used to transfer the capabilities of a large but powerful teacher model to a smaller but more efficient student model. Our method provides a direct mechanism for this: using reinforcement learning guided by the teacher’s endogenous reward.</p>
</div>
<div id="S7.SS3.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S7.SS3.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Instead of traditional distillation methods that force a student to mimic the teacher’s output, or RLAIF that uses the teacher to generate preference labels to train an intermediate reward model, our approach is more direct. We can extract the token-level endogenous reward signal directly from the teacher model’s logits and use it to fine-tune the student model via RL. This allows the student to explore and learn a policy that optimizes the teacher’s underlying principles and judgment, rather than merely imitating its surface-level outputs. This represents a more powerful form of teaching, potentially enabling smaller models to capture the nuanced capabilities of their larger counterparts more effectively.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S7.SS4" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">7.4 </span>Extending Reinforcement Learning Beyond Text</h3>

<div id="S7.SS4.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S7.SS4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The RLHF paradigm is heavily reliant on the feasibility of collecting human preference labels. While challenging for text, it is exponentially more difficult, expensive, and subjective for other modalities like images, video, or complex audio.</p>
</div>
<div id="S7.SS4.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S7.SS4.p2.1" class="ltx_p">The endogenous reward mechanism is not limited to language. <em id="S7.SS4.p2.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">It is applicable to any model with an auto-regressive structure.</em> This includes many state-of-the-art models for image generation, video synthesis, and music composition. As long as the model can process a prompt (which could be multi-modal) and generate a sequence of tokens, it can be prompted to evaluate its own output.</p>
</div>
<div id="S7.SS4.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S7.SS4.p3.1" class="ltx_p">This significantly widens the scope for applying reinforcement learning to align generative models in domains where RLHF is impractical, providing a scalable path to enhancing the quality and controllability of multimodal AI.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S7.SS5" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">7.5 </span>Limitations and Future Work</h3>

<div id="S7.SS5.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S7.SS5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">A critical area for future investigation is the potential for self-reinforcing biases. An endogenous reward system relies on the model’s internal worldview. If the base model contains undesirable biases learned from its training data, our method could inadvertently cause the model to amplify these biases during RL optimization. The model might reward itself for outputs that, while consistent with its learned patterns, are factually incorrect or socially harmful.</p>
</div>
<div id="S7.SS5.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S7.SS5.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Future work should explore hybrid approaches to mitigate this risk. For example, the endogenous reward could be used as the primary dense reward signal, while sparse but high-quality feedback from humans or a rule-based system could be used to provide periodic corrections, steering the model away from reinforcing its own flaws. Furthermore, research into the “prompt engineering” of the meta-question will be crucial for robustly and safely eliciting the desired reward signals.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S8" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">8 </span>Conclusion</h2>

<div id="S8.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S8.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In this work, we challenged the fundamental paradigm of LLM alignment, which has long relied on the costly development of external reward models. We introduced the concept of the <span id="S8.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">endogenous reward</span>, demonstrating that a generalist reward function is not something that needs to be built, but rather something that can be uncovered from within any pre-trained auto-regressive model.</p>
</div>
<div id="S8.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S8.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Our primary contribution is twofold. First, we provided a rigorous theoretical foundation for this claim, proving that the standard next-token prediction objective naturally yields a reward function that is equivalent to an offline inverse reinforcement learning algorithm. Second, we proved that using this elicited reward for RL fine-tuning is not merely a heuristic for avoiding data collection; it leads to a final policy with a provably tighter performance bound than the base model, effectively mitigating the compounding errors of simple imitation. Our empirical results confirmed these theoretical findings, showing state-of-the-art performance.</p>
</div>
<div id="S8.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S8.p3.1" class="ltx_p">The implications of this discovery suggest a future where the laborious and expensive reward modeling stage of the RLHF pipeline may become obsolete, replaced by a more direct and efficient process of self-improvement. It opens the door to dynamic, personalized alignment through promptable reward criteria and extends the reach of powerful RL techniques to multi-modal domains where collecting preference data is intractable. By revealing that the capacity for self-evaluation is an inherent property of generative models, we open the door to a more efficient, scalable, and autonomous future for aligning LLMs and other artificial intelligence models.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="Sx1" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section">Acknowledgments</h2>

<div id="Sx1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="Sx1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The authors would like to thank the Qwen team for providing the computational resources for experiments. This work was partially done when YC Li was an intern in the Qwen team. Google’s Gemini 2.5 Pro was employed to improve the language of this paper.</p>
</div>
</section>
